      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s courthouse first met in a private house before a dedicated building was erected in 1752, marking the start of Orange’s judicial history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1847 the Orange and Alexandria Railroad routed its line through Orange, turning the town into a rail hub for the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Orange’s strategic crossroads and the 1910 founding of the Orange Automobile Club made it a regional center for early automobile sales and services.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s historic core still hosts a restored 1730s house and a Hall of Agriculture showcasing early farm equipment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townoforangeva.gov/126/History",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Orange is the county seat of Orange County, located just 28 miles northeast of Charlottesville and 88 miles southwest of Washington, D.C.",
